WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.982
 

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:04.540
- Hello, in this video,

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:08.170
I'm going to be talking
about updates to webR's developer API

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:09.600
in webR 0.2.0.

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:14.530
So I'm going to jump straight
in there and talk about some

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:16.420
performance improvements
that we've made,

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:20.200
while using webR with other
JavaScript frameworks.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.770
So it is possible to work
entirely within webR and return

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:26.500
images directly from
webR graphics devices.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.070
But you might want to
also integrate webR into external

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:33.310
JavaScript frameworks,
including visualization packages.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.540
So there's an example workflow
shown on the screen there,

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:38.980
that you might want to do.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:41.200
So you would have to
initialize in webR.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.960
You can imagine that you
have some JavaScript data.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:48.760
And you want to get that data into R
so we can process it in some way. So

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.730
webR has APIs to do that.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:52.820
Here for example,

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.750
that JavaScript data is being
bound to a variable in the

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:58.600
global environment in the R--

00:00:58.600 --> 00:00:59.845
in the running R process.

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:05.360
Once that's been done and that data
is transferred to the R worker thread,

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:08.195
you can evaluate
some R code here.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:12.890
Just the function, do_analysis is being evaluated.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:14.900
But this could really
be anything in R.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:17.660
This could be something
quite complicated, such as

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.160
some kind of complicated data
manipulation with dplyr or some

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.990
kind of sophisticated
statistical analysis with tidymodels.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.140
In any case, once you've

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:34.250
done that computation,
you'll be returned--

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:36.275
an R object will
be returned to you.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:40.550
And then you can convert that
R object back into the JavaScript

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:42.980
environment with
this toJs method.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:47.090
That data is transferred from
the R worker environment back

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:48.260
to the JavaScript environment,

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:51.110
where you can use
it in some way.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:56.060
So if this is the kind
of workflow that as a webR

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:58.460
developer you'd
be interested in,

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:00.800
what we want to do
is make sure that,

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.090
that transferring of data
from the JavaScript environment into

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:08.720
R and back out of R
into the JavaScript environment is as

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:10.805
efficient and
performant as possible.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:13.970
And we've made some
changes in webR 0.2.0

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:16.110
to support that. And

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.960
that transfer was originally done
by encoding the data in a

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:21.785
format called JSON.

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:25.550
And that's been replaced
with something called the

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:26.705
MessagePack protocol,

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:31.035
which is binary-based
instead of text-based and

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:33.740
more efficient in other ways.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:36.890
Initial testing shows an
order of magnitude performance

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:39.950
improvement with this change. So
it was well worth looking

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:41.650
into,

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:45.350
especially when working with
large sets of JavaScript data.

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:52.630
So if you are working in
this way, an example now is shown

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:56.650
on the screen of what you can
do by moving data in and out of

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:57.700
the R environment.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:02:59.200
So here,

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:02.530
the start of this example
installs an R package just to

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:05.635
get some data. It's
installing the palmer penguins package.

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:10.270
Then the next part of the
example is, basically,

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:13.300
just grabbing that data. So
here, we're evaluating some R

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:13.960
code. But really,

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.585
we're just returning the
penguins data object.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.370
That's being converted into
JavaScript and then transferred

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:22.810
into a slightly
different format.

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:26.710
There's no real computation happening
here on lines 8 to

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:30.655
16. It's just changing the shape
of the data to better match

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:35.260
the data that is
understood by the Observable visualization

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.690
framework, a
JavaScript framework.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.150
If you have used
Observable before,

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:41.980
you probably recognize
these next lines.

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:43.855
They should be
fairly familiar. The

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:51.040
standard way to draw a
dot plot in Observable.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:55.150
So that can now take that
data that came from R,

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:59.440
that could have been
modified and computed on in a

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:00.295
sophisticated way

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:05.300
and then plot it in the
JavaScript native environment.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:07.750
And when you do that,
this is what you get.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:11.020
A plot that looks like this
produced by Observable,

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:13.300
but with the data
itself coming from R.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:14.530
So I think--

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:17.410
I like this example because
it demonstrates one of the real

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:19.720
benefits to web
technologies, in general,

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:23.590
which is interconnectivity. It's
very easy to flexibly

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:26.470
connect different projects together when
you're working on the web.

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:29.260
And I think this is a good
example of that when it comes to

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:31.000
joining webR

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:33.955
as part of a wider
JavaScript project.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:38.920
Another change we've made
is for TypeScript users.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:41.020
So when you're
working in TypeScript,

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.570
it's really important to pay
attention to the types of

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:47.395
objects. And that's true of objects
that are coming from R too.

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:49.510
In this example,

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:52.270
you can see that we're
just evaluating this string here,

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:54.445
which produces an
R double object.

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:57.670
Then we're running
a method toNumber,

00:04:57.670 --> 00:05:02.200
which exists on a webR
double object and just converts that

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:04.705
number into a JavaScript number.

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:07.810
Converts the double into
a JavaScript number.

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:10.030
And this is actually an error
under typescript, though.

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:12.190
And the reasoning
for that is that

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:14.070
evalR,

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:17.875
the function that evaluates
R code could evaluate anything.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:19.990
It doesn't have to
return a double.

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:23.410
It could return a character.
It could return a logical vector.

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:25.045
It could return
pretty much anything.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:29.930
So there's no guarantee that
this toNumber method exists

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:34.510
in the webR API to be
used on this R object.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:39.220
In fact, evalR is just
typed to return a R object.

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:43.555
That means it really could be
any kind of fundamental R data type.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:44.880
So

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:47.530
there's a couple of
ways to handle this.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:50.860
Before current release of webR,

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:54.700
the way we were
using was typescript type assertion

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:57.250
feature using this add
keyword. Basically,

00:05:57.250 --> 00:06:00.070
we just take the
result and we say, OK,

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:03.130
this object is definitely
a double. And therefore,

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:05.740
TypeScript is able to know
that you can use the toNumber

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:08.950
method on it, which, again,
exists in the webR API.

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:11.250
And

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:13.780
that works but
it has a problem.

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:16.090
It means that you need
to know, for definite,

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:19.300
what type of object
is returned by evalR.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:22.000
You need to know exactly what
R object is being returned.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:24.700
Otherwise, this is an
assertion, so it could be

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:27.670
wrong. I could be
returning a number here,

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:30.475
but setting it as an R
character. And that would cause issues

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:32.260
further down the line.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:38.640
So webR ships with a type
predicate functions. And here

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:40.830
is an example of one
of them isRDouble.

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:43.740
And similar functions exist
for other fundamental R object

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:45.840
types, isRCharacter, isRLogical.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:49.920
And what that allows you
to do is create a branch,

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:54.060
where the type of that object
is checked by isRDouble.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:58.560
And if the R object is
a double, that function returns true.

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:01.860
Because our function returns
true only in that case,

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:06.060
TypeScript is able to deduce
that inside this branch, the object

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:10.380
variable is definitely an
R double. And therefore,

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:12.990
it is perfectly fine
to use that toNumber

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:14.805
method that exists

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:16.095
on an R double.

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:19.620
And this is quite nice because
it means that you can return

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:21.090
different types of
object with evalR.

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:24.450
It doesn't have to just be
one particular type of R object.

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:26.700
And you could have different
branches and different if

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:28.845
statements to handle
different types of objects.

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:34.435
The next thing I'd like to
talk about is handling errors.

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:37.300
So one of the issues
that we have--

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:40.815
previous version of webR is that
if you have some R code

00:07:41.950 --> 00:07:42.450
that

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:46.930
causes an error
condition, the evalR

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:52.060
function automatically converts that
into a JavaScript error,

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:54.790
which is nice because it means
that you can handle errors in R

00:07:54.790 --> 00:07:57.340
code in the native
JavaScript environment.

00:07:58.030 --> 00:08:00.400
The issue with that is if you
have some kind of code like the

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:01.645
following, where you

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:06.220
evaluate some R code and then
do some JavaScript work on the result

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:09.760
and an error is thrown,

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:12.385
it's not entirely clear
where that error came from.

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:15.880
It could have come from inside
R or it could come from deep

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:17.710
inside this
JavaScript function.

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:21.355
And there are ways to handle
that. You could pass the message

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:25.660
to work out exactly
what the error means or

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:27.340
you could do other things.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:28.930
But they're not
particularly elegant.

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:32.439
So one of the changes
we've made is that

00:08:34.049 --> 00:08:35.309
errors from R now

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:38.055
are thrown as this webR error--

00:08:39.419 --> 00:08:41.474
as an instance of
this webR error class

00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:44.489
that exists in the package.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:49.350
So that means that you
can check the instance using the

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:52.110
instanceof keyword
in JavaScript to see,

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:53.940
is this object a webR error?

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:56.490
Or is it just some
other JavaScript error?

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:59.610
And then you can handle that
error as you like based on

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:02.490
whether it came from some R
code or it came from your

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:04.770
JavaScript code.
It's just a nice,

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:08.910
easy way to be able
to check exactly where your R--

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:11.265
where your error
condition came from.

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:19.260
We've made changes to the
webR Canvas messaging system. So

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:22.470
as I said in the first video,

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:27.090
we've upgraded the webR Canvas to
have a lot more support for

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:30.510
different features, including drawing

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:34.230
Canvas graphics on the worker
thread and then displaying them

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:37.230
on the main thread in a
different way, using offscreen

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:39.510
canvas. Due to those changes,

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:42.900
we've redesigned the messaging
system for the Canvas device.

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:46.020
So there are now a few
different messages.

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:50.280
So this message canvasNewPage
indicates that a new plot has

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:52.980
been created. Previously,
it was not clear

00:09:53.730 --> 00:09:56.640
when a new plot had been
created in webR when using the

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:57.720
Canvas graphics device.

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:01.740
And the canvasImage
event indicates that there's some

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:04.410
image data ready to be
shown on the main thread.

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:05.760
So the message looks like this.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:10.380
And the image property that's
returned in that message has a

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:12.165
JavaScript image bitmap object.

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:17.320
And that object can be written
to a Canvas element that's

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:18.850
on the main thread.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:21.160
So that message

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:24.220
will indicate that a
plot has been made.

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:26.155
It's finished rendering.
And it's ready to be shown.

00:10:29.470 --> 00:10:32.170
Another change we've made is how

00:10:32.890 --> 00:10:35.035
webR is terminated. So

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:39.400
the following example is
a useful pattern when you're

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:42.550
using webR to be
able to continuously handle webR

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:44.590
output. So here, for example,

00:10:44.590 --> 00:10:49.600
the read function is being used
with await to wait for some

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:50.980
output from R.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:53.410
So if you have some
kind of long running computation,

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:54.850
that will produce output

00:10:56.140 --> 00:10:56.845
after a while.

00:10:57.400 --> 00:10:59.020
And it will wait
for that output.

00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:00.625
It will wait for one of
those output messages.

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:04.180
Once the message
has been received,

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:06.835
it will be handled in some
way. So here, we have a switch

00:11:07.450 --> 00:11:09.520
statement to handle
different types of message.

00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:12.310
And then once that
message has been handled,

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:16.900
the loop wraps around. And
it waits for the next message.

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:20.920
And this loop is an infinite
loop. So it will continue on

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:24.190
indefinitely, continuously handling
messages from webR.

00:11:25.891 --> 00:11:27.880
And now a question arises.

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:31.270
It's possible to terminate a
webR session using this closed

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:33.880
function. And that will
close the worker thread,

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:34.870
terminate the thread,

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:41.410
and stop any further R
processing. And if you do that,

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:44.470
a question comes up of, OK,
well, inside the infinite loop,

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:46.255
how do I know when to stop?

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:50.050
That loop will just sit there
waiting for a message that may

00:11:50.050 --> 00:11:52.015
never be received or
will never be received.

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:53.520
So

00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:56.350
as part of the changes in
the new version of webR,

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:59.350
when webR is terminated
using this close function,

00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:01.090
a message of type
close is emitted.

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:04.855
The very last thing it does
is emit a message of type close.

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:09.820
That means you can add just a
single case statement in your switch here

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:11.890
to exit

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:14.440
your infinitely running loop

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:17.470
when that closed
statement is received.

00:12:17.470 --> 00:12:21.190
So that means that once the
webR thread terminates and then

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:22.780
message has been
been received,

00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:25.180
you don't have that function
sitting there waiting for a

00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:26.335
message that will never come.

00:12:27.620 --> 00:12:31.270
So it helps you clean up after
yourself when using webR.

00:12:32.596 --> 00:12:35.560
OK, so that's everything I want
to talk about in this video.

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:41.080
If you are interested in
the webR API, the documentation

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:43.540
lists a lot more than I
could fit in a single video.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:45.220
So do go and read that.

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:50.410
And you can install webR app from
NPM or you can import it from CDN.

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:53.950
And if you do use webR
in your own applications,

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:56.230
please do let me know
if you have any exciting

00:12:56.950 --> 00:12:58.420
features to show me.

00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:00.640
My email is just shown
on the screen there.

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:03.940
And I'd love to see what
you do with webR. Thank you.

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:05.740
[MUSIC PLAYING] PLAYING]

